WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.924 --> 00:00:14.233
Hi, Little Fox readers!

2
00:00:14.633 --> 00:00:15.574
It's Alex here.

3
00:00:16.475 --> 00:00:20.518
I just got back from a whole day of rock climbing with my dad.

4
00:00:21.319 --> 00:00:22.940
Boy, it was fun!

5
00:00:23.941 --> 00:00:29.405
When we got to the top of the tallest rock, there was a fantastic view of our town.

6
00:00:30.266 --> 00:00:32.128
We also spotted a hawk's nest!

7
00:00:33.224 --> 00:00:39.349
The climbing that Dad and I did today was pretty easy, but someday I want to climb some

8
00:00:39.529 --> 00:00:44.212
serious rock formations, like El Capitan or Half Dome.

9
00:00:45.173 --> 00:00:52.839
They're huge granite monoliths in Yosemite National Park, a landmark we learned about this week in Mr.

10
00:00:52.879 --> 00:00:53.679
Fay's class.

11
00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:00.004
The park is located in the Sierra Nevada, a mountain range in California.

12
00:01:00.808 --> 00:01:04.331
It's one of the oldest wilderness parks in the United States.

13
00:01:05.892 --> 00:01:10.236
Mr. Fay has been to Yosemite, and he showed our class some of his photos.

14
00:01:11.216 --> 00:01:21.504
We saw towering cliffs and waterfalls, deep valleys, fast-moving rivers, lakes, wild animals, mountain meadows, and giant trees.

15
00:01:22.585 --> 00:01:25.788
Of course, Yosemite wasn't always a national park.

16
00:01:26.708 --> 00:01:32.670
For thousands of years, Yosemite Valley was home to several groups of Native Americans.

17
00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:39.792
But in 1851 a group of white soldiers arrived to force them from the land.

18
00:01:40.512 --> 00:01:45.593
As word of Yosemite's incredible beauty spread, more people visited the valley.

19
00:01:46.614 --> 00:01:53.055
One of them was James Mason Hutchings, who believed he could make a lot of money running a

20
00:01:53.135 --> 00:01:55.256
hotel for adventurous tourists.

21
00:01:56.244 --> 00:02:00.306
Hutchings invited a photographer to the valley in 1859.

22
00:02:01.347 --> 00:02:09.211
Once the American public saw pictures of Yosemite's amazing landscape, the valley became a popular tourist destination.

23
00:02:10.652 --> 00:02:18.136
Mr. Fay told us the person who's most closely associated with Yosemite is John Muir, a naturalist.

24
00:02:19.297 --> 00:02:26.032
Muir arrived in Yosemite in 1868 and fell in love with the place, living there on and off

25
00:02:26.072 --> 00:02:27.473
for the next six years.

26
00:02:28.713 --> 00:02:36.895
Muir also grew concerned about the damage that grazing livestock had caused to Yosemite's grassy highlands and how

27
00:02:37.035 --> 00:02:39.456
quickly its forests were being chopped down.

28
00:02:40.256 --> 00:02:46.118
But by then Yosemite had an even bigger problem: tourists, and lots of them.

29
00:02:47.018 --> 00:02:52.600
Garbage was piling up in the valley, and people had carved tunnels through the trunks of some of

30
00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:53.720
the enormous trees.

31
00:02:54.972 --> 00:02:59.475
Muir wrote about the problems he saw, and he also urged the U.S.

32
00:02:59.535 --> 00:03:01.216
government to protect Yosemite.

33
00:03:02.376 --> 00:03:08.580
Thanks to Muir's determination, Yosemite National Park was established in 1890.

34
00:03:10.021 --> 00:03:15.103
Today over three million people visit Yosemite National Park each year.

35
00:03:15.784 --> 00:03:19.206
The most popular section of the park is Yosemite Valley.

36
00:03:20.146 --> 00:03:27.826
Many of Yosemite's famous waterfalls are here, including Yosemite Falls, the tallest waterfall in North America.

37
00:03:28.827 --> 00:03:34.249
If you go and see this waterfall during a full moon in spring, you might get to see

38
00:03:34.329 --> 00:03:35.170
a moonbow.

39
00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:41.573
That's just like a rainbow, except the colors are created by the light from the moon!

40
00:03:42.673 --> 00:03:52.395
Millions of years ago, massive glaciers sculpted this 13-kilometer valley, carving the granite into Yosemite's unique smooth domes

41
00:03:52.576 --> 00:03:53.576
and sheer walls.

42
00:03:54.677 --> 00:03:59.422
Rock climbers from all over the world come to Yosemite Valley to scale these walls.

43
00:04:01.003 --> 00:04:06.868
Mr. Fay showed us some photos of people climbing El Capitan—they were really high up!

44
00:04:07.709 --> 00:04:10.731
There was even a tent hanging halfway up the rock.

45
00:04:11.712 --> 00:04:17.645
Apparently most climbers need more than a day to climb the whole thing, so they set up bivouacs,

46
00:04:17.905 --> 00:04:21.448
or temporary campsites, right on the side of the wall!

47
00:04:22.529 --> 00:04:26.992
A lot of the big walls in Yosemite are only for advanced climbers.

48
00:04:27.832 --> 00:04:29.814
But beginners can have fun there too.

49
00:04:30.815 --> 00:04:35.118
A school offers rock-climbing classes for people of all different levels.

50
00:04:36.138 --> 00:04:37.419
When I asked Mr.

51
00:04:37.499 --> 00:04:43.904
Fay if he took a class, he said, "No way—I prefer to keep my feet on the ground!"

52
00:04:44.832 --> 00:04:50.395
To get to the top of Yosemite's monoliths, you don't have to be a rock climber; many of

53
00:04:50.455 --> 00:04:52.437
the mountains have hiking trails too.

54
00:04:53.917 --> 00:04:56.759
But that doesn't mean it's easy to reach the top!

55
00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:01.402
The trail up Half Dome is extremely difficult.

56
00:05:02.382 --> 00:05:10.247
In fact, the last 120 meters are so steep and slippery, you have to pull yourself up along

57
00:05:10.327 --> 00:05:11.588
some steel cables.

58
00:05:12.676 --> 00:05:17.960
Hundreds of people hike up Half Dome each day, and the trail gets crowded.

59
00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:22.423
So if you're brave enough to try it, start early!

60
00:05:23.403 --> 00:05:27.386
Another major attraction in Yosemite is Mariposa Grove.

61
00:05:28.386 --> 00:05:35.971
At heights exceeding fifty meters, the giant sequoias in this grove are some of the tallest trees in

62
00:05:36.011 --> 00:05:36.672
the world.

63
00:05:37.808 --> 00:05:42.489
I'll bet that walking along the trail through the grove makes people feel pretty tiny.

64
00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:50.372
One tree, the Grizzly Giant, is believed to be over two thousand years old.

65
00:05:51.572 --> 00:05:58.854
Another tree, known as the "Telescope tree," suffered severe fires and eventually became hollow.

66
00:05:59.854 --> 00:06:06.336
Amazingly, the tree is still alive—you can go inside it and see right up to the sky.

67
00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.721
The grove has a few trees that have fallen down too.

68
00:06:11.782 --> 00:06:19.264
Some of them fell more than two hundred years ago, but they're still intact because sequoias are extremely

69
00:06:19.344 --> 00:06:20.525
resistant to decay.

70
00:06:21.485 --> 00:06:24.726
Mr. Fay told us Yosemite is really crowded.

71
00:06:25.606 --> 00:06:29.768
Cars often clog the roads and tourists are everywhere.

72
00:06:30.852 --> 00:06:36.575
So during his visit, he hopped on to one of the park's free shuttle buses and went to

73
00:06:36.655 --> 00:06:37.735
Tuolumne Meadows.

74
00:06:39.476 --> 00:06:45.259
This area of the park receives fewer visitors than the valley, which makes it the perfect place to

75
00:06:45.299 --> 00:06:47.200
be alone and enjoy nature.

76
00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:53.983
You can sit and listen to the Tuolumne River, look at the mountains in the distance, wander through

77
00:06:54.023 --> 00:07:01.486
a field of wildflowers, or watch the sky for birds like Prairie falcons and Great gray owls.

78
00:07:02.607 --> 00:07:08.809
For people who don't want to climb the huge monoliths in the valley, Tuolumne Meadows has some smaller

79
00:07:08.849 --> 00:07:09.750
rock formations.

80
00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:15.592
They're much easier to climb but still provide some pretty impressive views.

81
00:07:16.592 --> 00:07:20.694
Well, it's time for me to get cleaned up and put away my climbing gear.

82
00:07:21.574 --> 00:07:25.096
Then I'm going to send Jake some of the photos I took today.

83
00:07:25.852 --> 00:07:28.614
Wait till he sees my photo of a cool snake!
